window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e"]={"zh":"<h1 id=\"permissions\">权限配置文件（Permissions）</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该页面的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>测试版：权限配置文件仍在积极开发中，后续可能发生变化。</p> <p>权限配置文件不能与旧版沙箱设置组合使用。请二选一进行配置：<code>default_permissions</code> 和 <code>[permissions]</code>，或 <code>sandbox_mode</code> / <code>sandbox_workspace_write</code>，不要同时配置两套。如果任何已加载的配置文件中出现 <code>sandbox_mode</code>、你传入了 <code>--sandbox</code>，或所选配置文件设置了 <code>sandbox_mode</code>，Codex 就会采用旧版沙箱设置，而不使用 <code>default_permissions</code>。</p> <p>托管设置 <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> 是例外：它会让 Codex 使用权限配置文件。部署托管的配置文件允许列表前，请移除 <code>sandbox_mode</code> 和 <code>[sandbox_workspace_write]</code> 等旧版设置。企业环境若要混合部署多个版本，可以暂时保留托管要求 <code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code> 作为兼容性限制，直到所有客户端都运行 Codex 0.138.0 或更高版本。</p> <p>权限配置文件可以为 Codex 代表你在本机运行的命令设置最小权限边界。配置文件是一种命名策略，其中组合了文件系统规则（规定命令可以读取或写入哪些内容）和网络规则（规定命令可以连接哪些目标）。</p> <p>将配置文件的 <code>network.enabled = true</code> 设为允许命令访问网络，但这不会启动网络代理。若要强制执行配置文件中的域名规则，还需启用 <code>features.network_proxy = true</code>，并在 <code>config.toml</code> 中设置此项，或使用已启用的管理员托管 <code>[experimental_network]</code> 要求。如果没有正在运行的代理，配置文件中的域名规则就不会限制直接网络访问。</p> <p>你可以用配置文件为 Codex 当前对话提供足够的访问权限，而不必开放对整台计算机或整个网络的广泛访问。例如，只读配置文件可以让 Codex 检查项目但不能编辑；可写配置文件则可以把编辑范围限制在指定的工作区根目录中。</p> <p>macOS、Linux、WSL 和原生 Windows 均支持本地权限配置文件。平台差异和注意事项请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e?anchor=scope-and-enforcement\">适用范围与强制执行</a>。</p> <p>当前 Codex Cloud 网络设置请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1?anchor=connect-to-services\">连接到服务</a>。Codex Cloud（旧版）的设置请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-b99b352f5f4ffa98\">互联网访问</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"define-and-select-a-profile\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_1\">定义并选择配置文件</h2> <p>Codex 内置了三个权限配置文件：</p> <ul> <li><code>:read-only</code>：本地命令只能以只读方式运行。</li> <li><code>:workspace</code>：允许在当前有效工作区根目录和系统临时目录中写入。</li> <li><code>:danger-full-access</code>：移除本地沙箱限制；只有在明确需要如此广泛的访问权限时才应使用。</li> </ul> <p>在 <code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;]</code> 下创建命名配置文件，然后将顶层键 <code>default_permissions</code> 设为该配置文件名称或上述某个内置名称。在此示例中，<code>project-edit</code> 是用户自定义的配置文件名称，不是内置值。</p> <p>企业管理员可以在托管的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中定义配置文件，并限制用户可选用的配置文件。一旦设置了 <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code>，未列出的配置文件都会被禁止，包括未列出的内置配置文件，以及未来 Codex 版本新增的配置文件。推荐的托管配置方法请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-available-permission-profiles\">控制可用的权限配置文件</a>。</p> <p>自定义配置文件涉及两个相关概念：</p> <ul> <li><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.workspace_roots]</code>：添加实际目录，将其纳入该配置文件的工作区根目录。</li> <li><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]</code>：定义 Codex 在每个有效工作区根目录中应用的文件系统规则。有效根目录包括当前会话运行时的工作区根目录，以及上面由配置文件定义的根目录。</li> </ul> <p>配置文件也遵循常规的配置层级模型。优先级较高的配置层可以在同一配置文件名称下添加或替换条目，而不必重新声明整个配置文件。</p> <p>例如，组织级配置和用户级配置可以分别扩展同一个配置文件：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># /etc/codex/config.toml\n[permissions.server.workspace_roots]\n\"~/code/server\" = true\n</code></pre> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># ~/.codex/config.toml\n[permissions.server.workspace_roots]\n\"~/code/mobile-app\" = true\n</code></pre> <p>启用 <code>server</code> 配置文件时，这两个工作区根目录都会纳入最终生效的配置文件。</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"project-edit\"\n\n[features]\nnetwork_proxy = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.workspace_roots]\n\"~/code/app\" = true\n\"~/code/shared-lib\" = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem]\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"write\"\n\".devcontainer\" = \"read\"\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network.domains]\n\"api.openai.com\" = \"allow\"\n\"objects.githubusercontent.com\" = \"allow\"\n\"*.github.com\" = \"allow\"\n\"tracking.example.com\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>此配置文件会：</p> <ul> <li>读取常见开发工具运行所需的最少路径。</li> <li>对当前会话的工作区根目录和配置文件定义的根目录应用相同规则。</li> <li>将每个根目录下 <code>.devcontainer/</code> 等 IDE 相关设置保持为只读。</li> <li>通过 glob 模式拒绝访问匹配的环境文件。</li> <li>只允许通过已配置的域名策略访问网络。</li> </ul> <p>在生效的配置文件中，即使较宽泛的路径规则允许读取或写入，更具体的拒绝规则仍会生效。例如，配置文件可以允许写入工作区根目录，同时仍将匹配的 <code>.env</code> 文件设为 <code>deny</code>。</p> <p><a id=\"extend-a-profile\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">扩展配置文件</h2> <p>如果某个配置文件的大部分规则都与内置配置文件或其他命名配置文件相同，请使用 <code>extends</code>。建议扩展内置配置文件，而不是从头创建，以便保留基础保护措施。例如，扩展 <code>:workspace</code> 后，工作区根目录下的 <code>.git</code>、<code>.agents</code>、<code>.codex</code> 和 <code>.aws</code> 路径仍为只读，除非你明确覆盖这些规则。只需指定一次父配置文件，然后添加或覆盖有差异的规则即可。</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"project-edit\"\n\n[features]\nnetwork_proxy = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit]\ndescription = \"Project editing with OpenAI API access.\"\nextends = \":workspace\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network.domains]\n\"api.openai.com\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>此配置文件以 <code>:workspace</code> 为基础，继续拒绝访问匹配的 <code>.env</code> 文件，并允许向 <code>api.openai.com</code> 发出请求。配置文件可以扩展 <code>:read-only</code>、<code>:workspace</code> 或其他命名配置文件，但不能扩展 <code>:danger-full-access</code>。Codex 也会拒绝未知的父配置文件和循环继承。</p> <p><a id=\"configuration-spec\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">配置项参考</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>配置项</th> <th>类型 / 可选值</th> <th>默认值</th> <th>说明</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>default_permissions</code></td> <td>配置文件名称（字符串）</td> <td>无</td> <td>指定 Codex 默认应用的权限配置文件。名称必须与 <code>[permissions]</code> 下的配置文件或 <code>:workspace</code> 等内置配置文件相符。为获得可预测的行为，应明确设置此项；只有在托管要求明确允许 <code>:workspace</code> 和 <code>:read-only</code> 两者时，才可以省略。除非托管的 <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> 要求此设置使用权限配置文件，否则 Codex 会使用旧版沙箱设置。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;]</code></td> <td>表</td> <td>无</td> <td>定义一个命名配置文件。<code>default_permissions</code> 用于选择默认配置文件；其他权限配置项也会使用该名称。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.description</code></td> <td>字符串</td> <td>无</td> <td>为配置文件提供便于阅读的说明。通过 <code>extends</code> 扩展时，不会继承父配置文件的说明。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.extends</code></td> <td>配置文件名称（字符串）</td> <td>无</td> <td>以另一个命名配置文件或内置的 <code>:read-only</code>、<code>:workspace</code> 为基础创建此配置文件。Codex 会拒绝 <code>:danger-full-access</code>、未知的父配置文件和循环继承。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.workspace_roots]</code></td> <td>表</td> <td>无</td> <td>添加由配置文件定义的工作区根目录；这些目录与当前会话运行时的工作区根目录一样，会应用 <code>:workspace_roots</code> 文件系统规则。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.workspace_roots.\"&lt;path&gt;\"</code></td> <td>布尔值</td> <td><code>false</code></td> <td>设为 <code>true</code> 时，将路径加入配置文件的工作区根目录集合。设为 <code>false</code> 的条目不会启用。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.filesystem]</code></td> <td>表</td> <td>无</td> <td>将文件系统路径映射到访问权限，或映射到按子路径划分的规则。若表缺失或为空，文件系统访问会保持受限，并在启动时发出警告。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.filesystem.glob_scan_max_depth</code></td> <td>数字</td> <td>无</td> <td>在 Linux、WSL 和原生 Windows 上，当 Codex 在沙箱启动前预先收集匹配项时，用此项限制 deny 读取规则的 glob 扩展深度。数值较大可能增加启动扫描工作量。若要限制无界模式的预展开范围，请至少设为 <code>1</code>，以便处理 <code>**</code> 模式。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.filesystem].\"&lt;path&gt;\"</code></td> <td><code>read</code>、<code>write</code> 或 <code>deny</code></td> <td>无</td> <td>为受支持的路径授予直接访问权限。<code>deny</code> 会拒绝访问，并优先于同等具体程度的 <code>write</code> 或 <code>read</code> 规则。如果当前运行时无法强制执行直接写入规则，Codex 会拒绝该规则。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.filesystem.\"&lt;path&gt;\"].\"&lt;subpath&gt;\"</code></td> <td><code>read</code>、<code>write</code> 或 <code>deny</code></td> <td>无</td> <td>为 <code>&lt;path&gt;</code> 下的子路径授予访问权限。基路径使用 <code>.</code>；其他子路径必须是相对后代路径，不能包含 <code>.</code> 或 <code>..</code> 路径段。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network]</code></td> <td>表</td> <td>无</td> <td>配置命令的网络访问权限，以及正在运行的网络代理要强制执行的策略。除非管理员托管的网络要求会启动代理，否则请启用 <code>features.network_proxy</code>。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.enabled</code></td> <td>布尔值</td> <td><code>false</code></td> <td>为此配置文件中的命令启用网络访问。此项不会启动网络代理；没有正在运行的代理时，命令可以不受域名限制地直接连接网络。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.domains]</code></td> <td>表</td> <td>无</td> <td>将主机模式映射为 <code>allow</code> 或 <code>deny</code>。规则仅在网络代理运行时生效。如果没有 <code>allow</code> 条目，代理会阻止域名请求；deny 条目优先于 allow 条目。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.domains.\"&lt;pattern&gt;\"</code></td> <td><code>allow</code> 或 <code>deny</code></td> <td>无</td> <td>支持精确主机名、用于子域名的 <code>*.example.com</code>、同时匹配主域和子域的 <code>**.example.com</code>，以及只能用于允许规则的全局通配符 <code>*</code>。主机模式会经过规范化：去除首尾空白、转为小写、删除末尾句点，并去掉端口号或方括号。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.unix_sockets]</code></td> <td>表</td> <td>无</td> <td>配置 Unix 套接字允许列表的覆盖规则。仅用于 Docker 等本地集成场景。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.unix_sockets.\"&lt;path&gt;\"</code></td> <td><code>allow</code> 或 <code>deny</code></td> <td>无</td> <td>使用 <code>allow</code> 将绝对 Unix 套接字路径加入最终允许列表，或使用 <code>deny</code> 拒绝该路径。被拒绝的条目不会进入最终允许列表。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.proxy_url</code></td> <td>URL 字符串</td> <td><code>http://127.0.0.1:3128</code></td> <td>HTTP 代理监听地址；供 <code>HTTP_PROXY</code>、<code>HTTPS_PROXY</code>、WebSocket 代理变量及相关工具代理环境变量使用。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.enable_socks5</code></td> <td>布尔值</td> <td><code>true</code></td> <td>启用供 <code>ALL_PROXY</code> 和 FTP 代理变量使用的 SOCKS5 监听器。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.socks_url</code></td> <td>URL 字符串</td> <td><code>http://127.0.0.1:8081</code></td> <td>SOCKS5 监听地址。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.enable_socks5_udp</code></td> <td>布尔值</td> <td><code>true</code></td> <td>启用 SOCKS5 监听器时，同时启用 SOCKS5 UDP 支持。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.allow_upstream_proxy</code></td> <td>布尔值</td> <td><code>true</code></td> <td>允许网络沙箱代理在出站请求时遵循上游 <code>HTTP(S)_PROXY</code> 和 <code>ALL_PROXY</code> 设置。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.allow_local_binding</code></td> <td>布尔值</td> <td><code>false</code></td> <td>设为 <code>true</code> 时会关闭本地/私有网络防护。设为 <code>false</code> 时，必须明确允许 <code>localhost</code> 或 <code>127.0.0.1</code> 等精确本地地址；解析到本地或私有 IP 的主机名仍会被阻止。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.dangerously_allow_non_loopback_proxy</code></td> <td>布尔值</td> <td><code>false</code></td> <td>允许代理监听器绑定到非回环地址。普通本地开发请不要设置此项。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.dangerously_allow_all_unix_sockets</code></td> <td>布尔值</td> <td><code>false</code></td> <td>在支持 Unix 套接字代理的环境中绕过套接字允许列表。这是范围很广的本地安全例外。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><a id=\"filesystem-permissions\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">文件系统权限</h2> <p>文件系统条目使用 <code>read</code>、<code>write</code> 或 <code>deny</code>：</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>权限</th> <th>含义</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>read</code></td> <td>允许命令读取该路径下的文件并列出目录内容；不能在其中创建、修改、重命名或删除文件。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>write</code></td> <td>允许命令读取并修改该路径下的文件；在操作系统允许的情况下，也可以创建、重命名和删除文件。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>deny</code></td> <td>拒绝读取和写入该路径。可用它从范围更广的 <code>read</code> 或 <code>write</code> 授权中划出禁止访问的子路径。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>更具体的条目会覆盖范围更广的条目。两个条目针对同一路径时，<code>deny</code> 优先于 <code>write</code>，而 <code>write</code> 优先于 <code>read</code>。</p> <p>利用这一优先级，配置文件可以先描述较大的工作区域，再划出必须保持不可读的文件或目录：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.filesystem]\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"write\"\n\".devcontainer\" = \"read\"\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>在此示例中，工作区根目录仍可写入，<code>.devcontainer/</code> 保持可读但不可写，而匹配的环境文件仍无法被沙箱命令访问。</p> <p>更具体的路径规则也可以在较宽泛的拒绝规则中重新开放一个较窄的子目录：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.filesystem]\n\"~/Documents\" = \"deny\"\n\"~/Documents/codex\" = \"write\"\n</code></pre> <p>支持以下路径形式：</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>路径</th> <th>含义</th> <th>支持限定子路径</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>:root</code></td> <td>文件系统根目录</td> <td>仅 <code>.</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>:minimal</code></td> <td>常用工具所需的平台和运行时路径</td> <td>仅 <code>.</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>:workspace_roots</code></td> <td>当前会话的工作区根目录，以及已启用的、由配置文件定义的工作区根目录</td> <td>支持</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>:tmpdir</code></td> <td><code>$TMPDIR</code> 指向的位置（如果可用）</td> <td>仅 <code>.</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>:slash_tmp</code></td> <td><code>/tmp</code> 目录（如果存在）</td> <td>仅 <code>.</code></td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>/absolute/path</code></td> <td>平台绝对路径，例如 macOS/Linux/WSL 上的 <code>/path</code>，或原生 Windows 上的 <code>C:\\path</code></td> <td>支持</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>~/path</code></td> <td>当前用户主目录下的路径</td> <td>支持</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>在原生 Windows 上，主目录相对路径也可以使用反斜杠，例如 <code>~\\work</code>。</p> <p>只有在配置文件确实需要广泛读取整个文件系统时，才使用 <code>:root</code>：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.audit.filesystem]\n\":root\" = \"read\"\n</code></pre> <p>使用 <code>:workspace_roots</code> 下的嵌套条目，将访问范围限制在相对于工作区根目录的子路径中：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"write\"          # each workspace root\n\"docs\" = \"read\"        # each workspace-root docs directory\n\"generated\" = \"deny\"   # each workspace-root generated directory\n</code></pre> <p>嵌套子路径必须位于各自的工作区根目录内。<code>../other-repo</code> 这类向上遍历路径会被拒绝。</p> <p><a id=\"deny-reads-with-exact-paths-or-globs\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"glob\">使用精确路径或 glob 模式拒绝读取</h3> <p>即使配置文件中的宽泛规则允许访问附近的路径，也可以对不应由 Codex 读取的文件或子目录使用 <code>deny</code>。对于 <code>~/.ssh</code> 等位置固定的路径，精确路径规则通常更合适；如果需要覆盖一类敏感文件，且它们在不同仓库中的具体位置不固定，则 glob 模式更合适。</p> <p>当 glob 模式位于 <code>:workspace_roots</code> 下时，Codex 会将其解释为相对于每个有效工作区根目录的路径。例如：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>此规则会拒绝读取每个运行时或配置文件定义的工作区根目录下匹配的 <code>.env</code> 文件。如果你希望保留工作区内的正常写入权限，同时让环境文件、生成的密钥或其他包含凭据的文件不可读，可以使用此规则。</p> <p>在 Linux、WSL，以及使用 MXC 或 <code>elevated</code> 模式的原生 Windows 上，每个沙箱化进程启动时都会枚举拒绝读取的 glob 模式所匹配的文件。进程启动后才创建的匹配文件，不会加入该进程的拒绝列表；如果该进程的其他权限允许访问，它仍可能读取这些文件。例如，长时间运行的 Shell、脚本、文件监视器或开发服务器，可能读取它启动后才创建的匹配 <code>.env</code> 文件。</p> <p>这份快照在该沙箱进程的整个生命周期内保持不变。通过 Codex 新启动一个沙箱化进程时会重新枚举匹配项；继续使用已经运行的进程则不会刷新拒绝列表。因此，不要依赖 glob 来阻止该进程读取启动后新建的文件。Windows 的具体限制请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-d86b339c4d76bce2?anchor=mxc-compatibility\">Windows MXC 兼容性说明</a>。</p> <p><code>deny</code> 的 glob 模式可用于拒绝读取。<code>read</code> 或 <code>write</code> 的 glob 模式在 Linux、WSL 和原生 Windows 沙箱中的可移植性较差；条件允许时，请优先使用精确路径或 <code>\"docs/**\" = \"read\"</code> 这类子目录规则。</p> <p>在 Linux、WSL 和原生 Windows 上，无界的 <code>**</code> 拒绝读取模式可能需要在沙箱启动前进行有界预展开。请设置 <code>glob_scan_max_depth</code> 来限制深度；例如，使用 <code>\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"</code> 这类模式时：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.filesystem]\nglob_scan_max_depth = 3\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p><code>glob_scan_max_depth</code> 必须至少为 <code>1</code>。数值越大，沙箱启动前扫描的目录层级越深，在 Linux、WSL 和原生 Windows 上可能增加启动开销。如果不想使用有界展开，也可以列出明确的层级，例如 <code>*.env</code>、<code>*/*.env</code> 和 <code>*/*/*.env</code>。</p> <p>如果相同规则还应应用于当前会话根目录之外的其他位置，请将可复用的工作区根目录加入配置文件：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.workspace_roots]\n\"~/code/app\" = true\n\"~/code/shared-lib\" = true\n</code></pre> <p>启用此配置文件后，Codex 会将 <code>:workspace_roots</code> 规则应用于当前会话运行时的工作区根目录，以及每个已启用的配置文件定义的工作区根目录。</p> <p>在原生 Windows 上，支持将 <code>D:\\work</code> 这类盘符路径和 <code>\\\\server\\share</code> 这类 UNC 路径用作绝对路径。</p> <p><a id=\"network-permissions\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">网络权限</h2> <p>网络访问与网络过滤是两个独立设置。将 <code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.enabled = true</code> 设为允许命令访问网络，并启用 <code>features.network_proxy</code> 来强制执行配置文件的域名规则：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[features]\nnetwork_proxy = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network.domains]\n\"example.com\" = \"allow\"      # exact host\n\"*.example.com\" = \"allow\"    # subdomains only\n\"**.example.com\" = \"allow\"   # apex and subdomains\n\"ads.example.com\" = \"deny\"   # deny wins over allow\n</code></pre> <p>实际行为取决于这两个设置：</p> <ul> <li><strong>关闭网络：</strong>无论代理功能是否开启，命令都不能访问网络。</li> <li><strong>开启网络、关闭代理：</strong>命令可以直接、不受限制地访问网络；权限配置文件中的域名规则不会强制执行。</li> <li><strong>开启网络、开启代理：</strong>命令通过代理访问网络，由代理强制执行配置文件的域名规则。如果正在运行的代理没有任何允许的域名，就会阻止访问外部目标。</li> </ul> <p>添加 <code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.domains]</code> 或设置 <code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.enabled = true</code> 都不会自动启用 <code>features.network_proxy</code>。管理员也可以通过 <code>[experimental_network]</code> 在 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中启用代理。详情请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access-requirements\">托管配置</a>。</p> <p>代理运行时，网络沙箱代理默认绑定到本地监听地址：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = true\nproxy_url = \"http://127.0.0.1:3128\"\nenable_socks5 = true\nsocks_url = \"http://127.0.0.1:8081\"\nenable_socks5_udp = true\n</code></pre> <p>除非要与特定运行时集成，否则请保留这些监听设置的默认值。<code>dangerously_*</code> 网络键是为特殊环境提供的安全例外，不适用于普通本地开发。</p> <p><a id=\"local-and-private-networks\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_6\">本地与私有网络</h3> <p>网络代理运行时，Codex 默认启用本地/私有网络防护，以抵御 DNS 重绑定和意外访问本地服务。若要有意允许某个明确的本地目标，请将精确主机名或 IP 字面量加入允许列表：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.network.domains]\n\"localhost\" = \"allow\"\n\"127.0.0.1\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>只有在配置文件必须访问解析到本地或私有地址的允许列表主机名时，才设置 <code>allow_local_binding = true</code>：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = true\nallow_local_binding = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network.domains]\n\"localhost\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"unix-sockets\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"unix\">Unix 套接字</h3> <p>Unix 套接字代理是为 Docker 等工具提供的本地安全例外，请谨慎使用：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.network.unix_sockets]\n\"/var/run/docker.sock\" = \"allow\"\n\"/tmp/old.sock\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>可以使用 <code>deny</code> 拒绝套接字路径，包括继承而来的允许条目。被拒绝的套接字路径不会出现在最终生效的允许列表中。</p> <p>启用 Unix 套接字时，请确保代理监听器仍绑定到回环地址。</p> <p><a id=\"migrate-from-older-sandbox-settings\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">从旧版沙箱设置迁移</h2> <p>如果希望通过一个可复用的配置文件同时描述文件系统和网络行为，权限配置文件可以替代旧版的 <code>sandbox_mode</code> 与 <code>sandbox_workspace_write</code> 组合。一个会话只能使用其中一套机制，不能两者并用。</p> <p>建议从以下方式开始：</p> <ul> <li><strong>只读工作流：</strong>使用内置 <code>:read-only</code> 配置文件，或自定义一个只在必要位置授予读取权限的配置文件。</li> <li><strong>编辑工作区：</strong>使用内置 <code>:workspace</code> 配置文件，或自定义一个配置文件； 通过 <code>:workspace_roots</code> 规则授予写权限，并且只额外开放工作流所需的临时目录或缓存路径。</li> <li><strong>不受限制的本地执行：</strong>只有在明确需要最广泛的本地访问模式时，才使用 <code>:danger-full-access</code>。</li> </ul> <p>配置文件描述的是会话在本机上的默认安全状态。组织托管要求仍可以增加限制，用户配置不得扩大这些限制。管理员强制执行的文件系统和网络约束请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">托管配置</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"scope-and-enforcement\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">适用范围与强制执行</h2> <p><a id=\"synced-work-and-environment-policy\"></a></p> <p><a id=\"local-computer-access-with-work-cloud-and-environment-policy\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"work-cloud\">Work Cloud 本地计算机访问与环境策略</h3> <p>请分别检查启用了 Work Cloud 本地计算机访问的每个环境所适用的策略：</p> <ul> <li><strong>企业要求：</strong>对于启用了本地访问和 Dots 的 Work，如果已启用托管策略，受支持的 Global 策略会约束共享云协调器；适用的本地执行要求则约束已连接的计算机。</li> <li><strong>云端执行：</strong>Work 云容器使用现有的 Work Cloud 策略。</li> <li><strong>本地策略顺序：</strong>MDM 和旧版托管设备要求的优先级高于 Agent Security；设备系统要求文件的优先级低于 Agent Security。</li> <li><strong>策略优先级：</strong>即使低优先级策略更加具体，仍以高优先级策略为准。在同一策略内，特定操作系统环境覆盖优先于适用于所有操作系统的环境覆盖，其后才是 Global。</li> </ul> <p>某些要求具有针对特定字段的合并规则。支持的字段和运行时限制请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">托管配置</a>和<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a>。不要假定 Work Cloud 支持本文展示的 Codex 权限配置文件。</p> <p>权限配置文件用于界定本地沙箱命令的执行范围。请将其与审批策略，以及网页搜索、连接器、MCP 服务器、内置浏览器、Computer Use 和 Codex Cloud 各自的独立控制配合使用。</p> <p><a id=\"what-profiles-control\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_9\">配置文件控制的内容</h3> <ul> <li><strong>本地命令执行：</strong>权限配置文件约束在本机运行的沙箱命令。连接器、MCP 服务器、浏览器或 Computer Use 界面、Codex Cloud 环境设置，以及获批的权限提升，都使用各自的控制机制。</li> <li><strong>文件系统写入：</strong>具备写入权限的配置文件可以造成持久更改。请谨慎对待脚本、构建步骤、包管理器钩子、Shell 启动文件和共享目录的写入，因为后续工具或用户可能在原沙箱上下文之外执行这些文件。</li> <li><strong>出站目标：</strong>只有在网络代理运行时，网络域名规则才会限制沙箱命令流量可以访问的目标。规则不会判断允许的目标是否可信；通配符允许规则仍然具有很宽的范围。</li> <li><strong>本地服务：</strong>网络代理运行时默认阻止本地和私有网络目标。 明确允许 <code>localhost</code>、私有 IP、Unix 套接字，或设置 <code>allow_local_binding = true</code>，即可开放对本地服务的访问。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"what-the-network-proxy-does-not-control\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_10\">网络代理无法控制的流量</h3> <p>网络代理只会筛选在沙箱中运行的本地命令产生的流量。配置文件的域名允许列表不会应用于以下流量：</p> <ul> <li><strong>网页搜索：</strong>托管搜索工具使用独立的访问设置。请使用 <code>web_search</code> 控制；托管客户端还可通过 <code>allowed_web_search_modes</code> 控制。 <code>tools.web_search.allowed_domains</code> 筛选的是搜索结果，不是命令的网络访问。</li> <li><strong>应用和连接器：</strong>由连接器提供的工具使用各自的服务端连接、工作区权限及应用或工具设置。</li> <li><strong>MCP 服务器：</strong>本地和远程 MCP 服务器使用各自的进程或传输方式。 请通过 <code>mcp_servers</code> 配置和托管服务器允许列表控制它们。</li> <li><strong>浏览器和 Computer Use：</strong>浏览器导航与计算机操作使用各自的功能开关和审批控制。</li> <li><strong>Codex 服务流量：</strong>模型、身份验证和其他客户端服务请求使用客户端独立的 HTTP 与系统代理设置。</li> <li><strong>Codex Cloud：</strong>这些任务使用其环境自己的 <a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1?anchor=connect-to-services\">网络设置</a>。 Codex Cloud（旧版）具有单独的 <a href=\"#/article/a-b99b352f5f4ffa98\">互联网访问设置</a>。</li> </ul> <p>若要限制这些功能，请分别配置每项功能。命令网络允许列表不是 Codex 所有操作通用的全局网络策略。</p> <p><a id=\"how-enforcement-works\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_11\">强制执行机制</h3> <ul> <li><strong>macOS：</strong>Codex 使用 Seatbelt 沙箱配置文件。如果平台沙箱无法强制执行所选策略，Codex 会拒绝运行命令，而不会悄悄在无沙箱状态下运行。</li> <li><strong>Linux 和 WSL：</strong>Codex 使用 <a href=\"https://github.com/containers/bubblewrap\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">bubblewrap（需联网）</a> 和 <a href=\"https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/userspace-api/seccomp_filter.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">seccomp（需联网）</a> 强制执行文件系统限制。旧版 Landlock 选项无法隔离 app-server Unix 套接字，因此会被文件系统受限策略拒绝。升级时请禁用 <code>features.use_legacy_landlock</code>。如果主机阻止了 bubblewrap 所需操作，就无法运行这些策略。</li> <li><strong>Windows：</strong>MXC 使用进程隔离来实施文件系统和网络权限。 启用前请查看 <a href=\"#/article/a-d86b339c4d76bce2?anchor=mxc-compatibility\">MXC 兼容性说明</a>。 旧版 <code>elevated</code> 回退方案使用专用的低权限沙箱用户、文件系统权限边界和防火墙规则。 <code>unelevated</code> 是网络隔离较弱的旧版回退方案，且无法强制执行所有读写权限拆分，因此不支持的策略会被拒绝。如果需要 Linux 沙箱模型，请使用 WSL。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"operational-guidance\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_12\">运维建议</h3> <p>请选择足以完成任务的最窄权限配置文件，尤其是在授予写入权限或出站网络访问时。审批策略、密钥处理方式和允许规则都应与该访问级别保持一致。</p> <p><a id=\"common-profiles\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_13\">常见配置文件</h2> <p><a id=\"read-only-with-network-allowlist\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_14\">只读并允许访问指定网络</h3> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"readonly-net\"\n\n[features]\nnetwork_proxy = true\n\n[permissions.readonly-net.filesystem]\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.readonly-net.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.readonly-net.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[permissions.readonly-net.network.domains]\n\"api.openai.com\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"file-access-limited-to-workspace\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_15\">将文件访问限制在工作区</h3> <p>以下示例会允许 Codex 写入工作区文件夹，同时拒绝读取文件系统的其余部分；<code>:minimal</code> 所需的少量例外除外。</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"workspace-only\"\n\n[permissions.workspace-only]\n# By extending the :workspace profile, you get Codex's safeguards to ensure\n# paths such as .git/, .agents/, .codex/, and .aws/ within a workspace root\n# are read-only while the rest of the folder is writable.\nextends = \":workspace\"\n\n[permissions.workspace-only.filesystem]\n# By default, deny read access to all files on disk.\n\":root\" = \"deny\"\n\n# Though in practice, a software agent needs to be able to read folders that\n# contain common tools, such as `/usr/bin`, to get work done, so grant access\n# to a \"minimal\" set of files and folders, as determined by Codex.\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n# By extending the :workspace profile, :tmpdir and :slash_tmp are \"write\" by\n# default, though you can deny access to them altogether, if desired.\n\":tmpdir\" = \"deny\"\n\":slash_tmp\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"workspace-write-without-network\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_16\">允许写入工作区，但关闭网络</h3> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"project-edit\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem]\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"write\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = false\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"workspace-write-with-public-web-access\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_17\">允许写入工作区并访问公共网络</h3> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"workspace-net\"\n\n[features]\nnetwork_proxy = true\n\n[permissions.workspace-net.filesystem]\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.workspace-net.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"write\"\n\n[permissions.workspace-net.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[permissions.workspace-net.network.domains]\n\"*\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>只有在确实要允许访问公共网络时，才使用全局 <code>\"*\"</code> 允许规则。你可以通过拒绝规则缩小宽泛允许列表的范围。</p>","en":"<h1 id=\"permissions\">Permissions</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>Beta. Permission profiles are under active development and may change.</p> <p>Permission profiles do not compose with the older sandbox settings. Configure either <code>default_permissions</code> and <code>[permissions]</code>, or <code>sandbox_mode</code> / <code>sandbox_workspace_write</code>, but not both. If <code>sandbox_mode</code> appears in any loaded config file, you pass <code>--sandbox</code>, or the selected config profile sets <code>sandbox_mode</code>, Codex uses those older sandbox settings instead of <code>default_permissions</code>.</p> <p>Managed <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> is the exception: it makes Codex use permission profiles. Remove older settings such as <code>sandbox_mode</code> and <code>[sandbox_workspace_write]</code> before deploying a managed profile allowlist. For a mixed-version enterprise rollout, you can keep the managed <code>allowed_sandbox_modes</code> requirement as a temporary compatibility constraint until every client runs Codex 0.138.0 or later.</p> <p>Permission profiles let you apply least-privilege boundaries to local commands Codex runs on your behalf. A profile is a named policy that combines filesystem rules, which define what commands can read or write, with network rules, which define which destinations commands can reach.</p> <p>A profile's <code>network.enabled = true</code> permits command network access, but it does not start the network proxy. To enforce profile domain rules, also set <code>features.network_proxy = true</code> in <code>config.toml</code>, or use enabled, administrator-managed <code>[experimental_network]</code> requirements. Without an active proxy, profile domain rules do not restrict direct network access.</p> <p>Use profiles to give Codex enough access for the current chat without granting broad access to your machine or network. For example, a read-only profile can let Codex inspect a project without editing it, while a write-capable profile can limit edits to selected workspace roots.</p> <p>Local permission profiles are supported on macOS, Linux, WSL, and native Windows. See <a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e?anchor=scope-and-enforcement\">Scope and enforcement</a> for platform-specific details and caveats.</p> <p>For current Codex Cloud network settings, see <a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1?anchor=connect-to-services\">Connect to services</a>. For Codex Cloud (Legacy), see <a href=\"#/article/a-b99b352f5f4ffa98\">Internet access</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"define-and-select-a-profile\">Define and select a profile</h2> <p>Codex includes three built-in permission profiles:</p> <ul> <li><code>:read-only</code> keeps local command execution read-only.</li> <li><code>:workspace</code> allows writes inside the active workspace roots and system temp directories.</li> <li><code>:danger-full-access</code> removes local sandbox restrictions and should be used only when that broad access is intentional.</li> </ul> <p>Create a named profile under <code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;]</code>, then set the top-level <code>default_permissions</code> key to that profile name or to one of the built-ins above. In this example, <code>project-edit</code> is a user-defined profile name, not a built-in value.</p> <p>Enterprise administrators can define profiles and restrict which profiles users may select through managed <code>requirements.toml</code>. Once <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> is present, omitted profiles are denied, including omitted built-ins and profiles added in future Codex versions. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-available-permission-profiles\">Control available permission profiles</a> for the recommended managed configuration.</p> <p>Custom profiles use two related concepts:</p> <ul> <li><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.workspace_roots]</code> adds concrete directories that should count as workspace roots for that profile.</li> <li><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]</code> defines the filesystem rules Codex applies inside every effective workspace root: the current session's runtime workspace roots plus the profile-defined roots above.</li> </ul> <p>Profiles also use the normal config-layer model. Higher-precedence layers can add or replace entries under the same profile name without restating the whole profile.</p> <p>For example, an organization-level config and a user-level config can extend the same profile independently:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># /etc/codex/config.toml\n[permissions.server.workspace_roots]\n\"~/code/server\" = true\n</code></pre> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># ~/.codex/config.toml\n[permissions.server.workspace_roots]\n\"~/code/mobile-app\" = true\n</code></pre> <p>When <code>server</code> is active, both workspace roots participate in the effective profile.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"project-edit\"\n\n[features]\nnetwork_proxy = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.workspace_roots]\n\"~/code/app\" = true\n\"~/code/shared-lib\" = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem]\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"write\"\n\".devcontainer\" = \"read\"\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network.domains]\n\"api.openai.com\" = \"allow\"\n\"objects.githubusercontent.com\" = \"allow\"\n\"*.github.com\" = \"allow\"\n\"tracking.example.com\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>This profile:</p> <ul> <li>Reads the minimal runtime paths common developer tools need.</li> <li>Applies the same workspace-root rules to the current session and the profile-defined roots.</li> <li>Keeps IDE-adjacent settings such as <code>.devcontainer/</code> read-only under each root.</li> <li>Denies matching environment files with a glob rule.</li> <li>Allows network access only through the configured domain policy.</li> </ul> <p>Inside an active profile, narrower deny rules stay in force even when a broader path is readable or writable. For example, a profile can make workspace roots writable while still setting a matching <code>.env</code> path to <code>deny</code>.</p> <h2 id=\"extend-a-profile\">Extend a profile</h2> <p>Use <code>extends</code> when a profile is mostly the same as a built-in or another named profile. Prefer extending a built-in profile over starting from scratch so baseline protections carry forward. Extending <code>:workspace</code>, for example, keeps the workspace root's <code>.git</code>, <code>.agents</code>, <code>.codex</code>, and <code>.aws</code> paths read-only unless you explicitly override those rules. Set the parent once, then add or override only the rules that differ.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"project-edit\"\n\n[features]\nnetwork_proxy = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit]\ndescription = \"Project editing with OpenAI API access.\"\nextends = \":workspace\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network.domains]\n\"api.openai.com\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>This profile starts with <code>:workspace</code>, keeps matching <code>.env</code> files denied, and allows requests to <code>api.openai.com</code>. A profile can extend <code>:read-only</code>, <code>:workspace</code>, or another named profile. It cannot extend <code>:danger-full-access</code>; Codex also rejects unknown parents and inheritance cycles.</p> <h2 id=\"configuration-spec\">Configuration spec</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Entry</th> <th>Type / values</th> <th>Default</th> <th>Details</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>default_permissions</code></td> <td>String profile name</td> <td>None</td> <td>Names the permissions profile Codex applies by default. It must match a profile under <code>[permissions]</code> or a built-in such as <code>:workspace</code>. Set it explicitly for predictable behavior; managed requirements may omit it only when both <code>:workspace</code> and <code>:read-only</code> are explicitly allowed. Codex uses older sandbox settings unless managed <code>allowed_permission_profiles</code> tells it to use permission profiles in this setup.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;]</code></td> <td>Table</td> <td>None</td> <td>Defines a named profile. <code>default_permissions</code> selects one profile as the default; other permission-profile settings also use the profile name.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.description</code></td> <td>String</td> <td>None</td> <td>Provides a human-readable description for the profile. A profile does not inherit its parent's description through <code>extends</code>.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.extends</code></td> <td>String profile name</td> <td>None</td> <td>Starts this profile from another named profile or the built-in <code>:read-only</code> or <code>:workspace</code> profile. Codex rejects <code>:danger-full-access</code>, unknown parents, and inheritance cycles.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.workspace_roots]</code></td> <td>Table</td> <td>None</td> <td>Adds profile-defined workspace roots that receive <code>:workspace_roots</code> filesystem rules alongside the current session's runtime workspace roots.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.workspace_roots.\"&lt;path&gt;\"</code></td> <td>boolean</td> <td><code>false</code></td> <td>Adds the path to the profile's workspace root set when <code>true</code>. Entries set to <code>false</code> remain inactive.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.filesystem]</code></td> <td>Table</td> <td>None</td> <td>Maps filesystem paths to access values or scoped subpath maps. Missing or empty filesystem tables keep filesystem access restricted and emit a startup warning.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.filesystem.glob_scan_max_depth</code></td> <td>Number</td> <td>None</td> <td>Limits deny-read glob expansion on Linux, WSL, and native Windows when Codex snapshots matches before sandbox startup. Larger values can increase startup scanning work. Use a value of at least <code>1</code> when an unbounded <code>**</code> pattern needs bounded pre-expansion.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.filesystem].\"&lt;path&gt;\"</code></td> <td><code>read</code>, <code>write</code>, or <code>deny</code></td> <td>None</td> <td>Grants direct access for a supported path. <code>deny</code> denies access and wins over equally specific <code>write</code> or <code>read</code> entries. Codex rejects direct write rules that the active runtime cannot enforce.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.filesystem.\"&lt;path&gt;\"].\"&lt;subpath&gt;\"</code></td> <td><code>read</code>, <code>write</code>, or <code>deny</code></td> <td>None</td> <td>Grants access to a descendant of <code>&lt;path&gt;</code>. Use <code>.</code> for the base path. Other subpaths must be relative descendants and cannot contain <code>.</code> or <code>..</code> components.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network]</code></td> <td>Table</td> <td>None</td> <td>Configures command network access and the policy that an active network proxy enforces. Enable <code>features.network_proxy</code> unless administrator-managed network requirements start the proxy.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.enabled</code></td> <td>boolean</td> <td><code>false</code></td> <td>Enables network access for commands in the profile. It does not start the network proxy; without an active proxy, commands can connect directly without domain restrictions.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.domains]</code></td> <td>Table</td> <td>None</td> <td>Maps host patterns to <code>allow</code> or <code>deny</code>. Rules apply only when the network proxy is active. The active proxy blocks domain requests if there are no <code>allow</code> entries, and deny entries override allow entries.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.domains.\"&lt;pattern&gt;\"</code></td> <td><code>allow</code> or <code>deny</code></td> <td>None</td> <td>Supports exact hosts, <code>*.example.com</code> for subdomains, <code>**.example.com</code> for apex plus subdomains, and <code>*</code> as an allow-only global wildcard. Host patterns are normalized by trimming, converting to lowercase, stripping a trailing dot, and stripping port numbers or brackets.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.unix_sockets]</code></td> <td>Table</td> <td>None</td> <td>Maps Unix socket allowlist overrides. Use only for local integrations such as Docker.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.unix_sockets.\"&lt;path&gt;\"</code></td> <td><code>allow</code> or <code>deny</code></td> <td>None</td> <td>Adds an absolute Unix socket path to the effective allowlist with <code>allow</code>, or rejects it with <code>deny</code>. Denied entries are omitted from the effective allowlist.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.proxy_url</code></td> <td>URL string</td> <td><code>http://127.0.0.1:3128</code></td> <td>HTTP proxy listener used for <code>HTTP_PROXY</code>, <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code>, WebSocket proxy variables, and related tool proxy environment variables.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.enable_socks5</code></td> <td>boolean</td> <td><code>true</code></td> <td>Enables the SOCKS5 listener used for <code>ALL_PROXY</code> and FTP proxy variables.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.socks_url</code></td> <td>URL string</td> <td><code>http://127.0.0.1:8081</code></td> <td>SOCKS5 listener address.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.enable_socks5_udp</code></td> <td>boolean</td> <td><code>true</code></td> <td>Enables SOCKS5 UDP support when the SOCKS5 listener is enabled.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.allow_upstream_proxy</code></td> <td>boolean</td> <td><code>true</code></td> <td>Allows the network sandbox proxy to respect upstream <code>HTTP(S)_PROXY</code> and <code>ALL_PROXY</code> settings for outbound requests.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.allow_local_binding</code></td> <td>boolean</td> <td><code>false</code></td> <td>Disables the local/private-network guard when <code>true</code>. When <code>false</code>, exact local literals such as <code>localhost</code> or <code>127.0.0.1</code> must be explicitly allowlisted, and hostnames that resolve to local or private IPs remain blocked.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.dangerously_allow_non_loopback_proxy</code></td> <td>boolean</td> <td><code>false</code></td> <td>Allows proxy listeners to bind non-loopback addresses. Leave unset for ordinary local development.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.dangerously_allow_all_unix_sockets</code></td> <td>boolean</td> <td><code>false</code></td> <td>Bypasses the Unix socket allowlist where Unix socket proxying is supported. This is a broad local escape hatch.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2 id=\"filesystem-permissions\">Filesystem permissions</h2> <p>Filesystem entries use <code>read</code>, <code>write</code>, or <code>deny</code>:</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Access</th> <th>Meaning</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>read</code></td> <td>Allows commands to read files and list directories under the path. Commands cannot create, modify, rename, or delete files there.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>write</code></td> <td>Allows commands to read and modify files under the path, including creating, renaming, and deleting files when the OS allows it.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>deny</code></td> <td>Denies both reads and writes under the path. Use it to carve out a denied subpath from a broader <code>read</code> or <code>write</code> grant.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>More specific entries override broader entries. When two entries target the same path, <code>deny</code> takes precedence over <code>write</code>, and <code>write</code> takes precedence over <code>read</code>.</p> <p>This precedence lets a profile describe a broad working area first, then carve out files or directories that should stay unreadable:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.filesystem]\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"write\"\n\".devcontainer\" = \"read\"\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>In this example, the workspace root stays writable, <code>.devcontainer/</code> stays readable without becoming writable, and matching environment files remain unavailable to sandboxed commands.</p> <p>A more specific path can also reopen a narrower subtree inside a broader deny:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.filesystem]\n\"~/Documents\" = \"deny\"\n\"~/Documents/codex\" = \"write\"\n</code></pre> <p>Supported path forms:</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Path</th> <th>Meaning</th> <th>Scoped subpaths</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>:root</code></td> <td>The filesystem root</td> <td><code>.</code> only</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>:minimal</code></td> <td>Platform and runtime paths needed by common tools</td> <td><code>.</code> only</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>:workspace_roots</code></td> <td>The current session's workspace roots plus any enabled profile-defined workspace roots</td> <td>Yes</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>:tmpdir</code></td> <td>The <code>$TMPDIR</code> location, when one is available</td> <td><code>.</code> only</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>:slash_tmp</code></td> <td>The <code>/tmp</code> folder, if it exists</td> <td><code>.</code> only</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>/absolute/path</code></td> <td>A platform absolute path, such as <code>/path</code> on macOS/Linux/WSL or <code>C:\\path</code> on native Windows</td> <td>Yes</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>~/path</code></td> <td>A path under the current user's home directory</td> <td>Yes</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>On native Windows, home-relative paths can also use backslashes, such as <code>~\\work</code>.</p> <p>Use <code>:root</code> only when a profile intentionally needs broad read coverage:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.audit.filesystem]\n\":root\" = \"read\"\n</code></pre> <p>Use nested entries under <code>:workspace_roots</code> to scope access to workspace-root relative subpaths:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"write\"          # each workspace root\n\"docs\" = \"read\"        # each workspace-root docs directory\n\"generated\" = \"deny\"   # each workspace-root generated directory\n</code></pre> <p>Nested subpaths must stay inside their workspace root. Parent traversal such as <code>../other-repo</code> is rejected.</p> <h3 id=\"deny-reads-with-exact-paths-or-globs\">Deny reads with exact paths or globs</h3> <p>Use <code>deny</code> for files or subtrees that Codex should not read, even when a broader profile rule grants access nearby. Exact paths work well for stable locations such as <code>~/.ssh</code>. Glob patterns work better when a profile needs to cover a family of sensitive files whose exact locations vary across repositories.</p> <p>When a glob sits under <code>:workspace_roots</code>, Codex interprets it relative to each effective workspace root. For example:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>This rule denies reads for matching <code>.env</code> files found beneath each runtime or profile-defined workspace root. Use it when you want to preserve normal workspace writes while keeping environment files, generated secrets, or similar credential-bearing files unreadable.</p> <p>On Linux, WSL, and native Windows with MXC or <code>elevated</code>, deny-read glob matches are enumerated when each sandboxed process starts. Matching files created afterward are not added to that process's deny list and remain readable if its other permissions allow access. For example, a long-running shell, script, watcher, or development server can read a matching <code>.env</code> file created after it started.</p> <p>This snapshot lasts for the sandboxed process's lifetime, not the whole Codex session. Starting a new sandboxed process through Codex enumerates the matches again; continuing to use an already-running process does not refresh its deny list. Don't rely on a glob to block newly created files in that running process. See <a href=\"#/article/a-d86b339c4d76bce2?anchor=mxc-compatibility\">Windows MXC compatibility</a> for Windows-specific limits.</p> <p><code>deny</code> glob patterns are supported as deny-read rules. <code>read</code> or <code>write</code> globs are less portable on Linux, WSL, and native Windows sandboxing, so prefer exact paths or subtree rules such as <code>\"docs/**\" = \"read\"</code> when possible.</p> <p>On Linux, WSL, and native Windows, an unbounded <code>**</code> deny-read pattern may need bounded pre-expansion before the sandbox starts. Set <code>glob_scan_max_depth</code> when you use an unbounded pattern such as <code>\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"</code>:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.filesystem]\nglob_scan_max_depth = 3\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\"**/*.env\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p><code>glob_scan_max_depth</code> must be at least <code>1</code>. Higher values scan deeper before sandbox startup, which can add startup work on Linux, WSL, and native Windows. If you prefer not to use bounded expansion, list explicit depths such as <code>*.env</code>, <code>*/*.env</code>, and <code>*/*/*.env</code>.</p> <p>Add reusable workspace roots to the profile when the same rules should apply to more than the current session root:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.workspace_roots]\n\"~/code/app\" = true\n\"~/code/shared-lib\" = true\n</code></pre> <p>When this profile is active, Codex applies the <code>:workspace_roots</code> rules to the current session's runtime workspace roots and to each enabled profile-defined workspace root.</p> <p>On native Windows, drive-letter paths such as <code>D:\\work</code> and UNC paths such as <code>\\\\server\\share</code> are supported as absolute paths.</p> <h2 id=\"network-permissions\">Network permissions</h2> <p>Network access and network filtering are separate settings. Set <code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.enabled = true</code> to let commands access the network, and enable <code>features.network_proxy</code> to enforce the profile's domain rules:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[features]\nnetwork_proxy = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network.domains]\n\"example.com\" = \"allow\"      # exact host\n\"*.example.com\" = \"allow\"    # subdomains only\n\"**.example.com\" = \"allow\"   # apex and subdomains\n\"ads.example.com\" = \"deny\"   # deny wins over allow\n</code></pre> <p>The resulting behavior depends on both settings:</p> <ul> <li>Network off: Commands cannot access the network, regardless of the proxy feature.</li> <li>Network on, proxy off: Commands have direct, unrestricted network access. Domain rules in the permission profile are not enforced.</li> <li>Network on, proxy on: Commands use the proxy, which enforces the profile's domain rules. If the active proxy has no allowed domains, it blocks external destinations.</li> </ul> <p>Adding <code>[permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.domains]</code> or setting <code>permissions.&lt;name&gt;.network.enabled = true</code> does not enable <code>features.network_proxy</code>. As an alternative, administrators can enable the proxy with <code>[experimental_network]</code> in <code>requirements.toml</code>. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access-requirements\">Managed configuration</a>.</p> <p>When active, the network sandbox proxy binds to local listeners by default:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = true\nproxy_url = \"http://127.0.0.1:3128\"\nenable_socks5 = true\nsocks_url = \"http://127.0.0.1:8081\"\nenable_socks5_udp = true\n</code></pre> <p>Leave these listener settings at their defaults unless you are integrating with a specific runtime. The <code>dangerously_*</code> network keys are escape hatches for specialized environments and should not be used for ordinary local development.</p> <h3 id=\"local-and-private-networks\">Local and private networks</h3> <p>When the network proxy is active, Codex applies a local/private-network guard by default as a defense against DNS rebinding and accidental access to local services. To intentionally allow a literal local target, allowlist the exact host or IP literal:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.network.domains]\n\"localhost\" = \"allow\"\n\"127.0.0.1\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>Set <code>allow_local_binding = true</code> only when the profile must reach allowlisted hostnames that resolve to local or private addresses:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = true\nallow_local_binding = true\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network.domains]\n\"localhost\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <h3 id=\"unix-sockets\">Unix sockets</h3> <p>Unix socket proxying is a local escape hatch for tools such as Docker. Use it sparingly:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">[permissions.project-edit.network.unix_sockets]\n\"/var/run/docker.sock\" = \"allow\"\n\"/tmp/old.sock\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <p>Use <code>deny</code> to reject a socket path, including an inherited allow entry. Denied socket paths are omitted from the effective allowlist.</p> <p>When Unix sockets are enabled, keep proxy listeners bound to loopback addresses.</p> <h2 id=\"migrate-from-older-sandbox-settings\">Migrate from older sandbox settings</h2> <p>Permission profiles replace the older combination of <code>sandbox_mode</code> and <code>sandbox_workspace_write</code> when you want one reusable profile to describe both filesystem and network behavior. Use one system or the other for a session, not both.</p> <p>Suggested starting points:</p> <ul> <li>For a read-only workflow, use the built-in <code>:read-only</code> profile or define a custom profile with read access only where needed.</li> <li>For workspace editing, use the built-in <code>:workspace</code> profile or define a custom profile that writes through <code>:workspace_roots</code> and adds only the extra temp or cache paths the workflow needs.</li> <li>For unrestricted local execution, use <code>:danger-full-access</code> only when you intentionally want the broadest local access model.</li> </ul> <p>Profiles describe the local default posture for a session. Organization-managed requirements can still add restrictions that user configuration should not broaden. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a> for admin-enforced filesystem and network constraints.</p> <h2 id=\"scope-and-enforcement\">Scope and enforcement</h2> <p><a id=\"synced-work-and-environment-policy\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"local-computer-access-with-work-cloud-and-environment-policy\">Local computer access with Work Cloud and environment policy</h3> <p>Review the policy for each environment where Local computer access with Work Cloud runs:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Enterprise requirements:</strong> For Work with local access and dots, supported Global policy governs the shared cloud orchestrator when managed policy is enabled; applicable local execution requirements govern the connected computer.</li> <li><strong>Cloud execution:</strong> Work cloud containers use existing Work Cloud policies.</li> <li><strong>Local policy order:</strong> MDM and legacy managed-device requirements rank above Agent Security. The device's system requirements file ranks below Agent Security.</li> <li><strong>Policy priority:</strong> A higher-priority policy wins even if a lower-priority policy is more specific. Within one policy, OS-specific environment overrides take priority over all-OS environment overrides, followed by Global.</li> </ul> <p>Some requirements have field-specific merge rules. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a> and the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration Reference</a> for supported fields and runtime limits. Don't assume Work Cloud supports the Codex permission profiles shown here.</p> <p>Permission profiles define the boundaries for local sandboxed command execution. Use them together with approval policies and the separate controls for web search, connectors, MCP servers, the built-in browser, Computer Use, and Codex cloud.</p> <h3 id=\"what-profiles-control\">What profiles control</h3> <ul> <li><strong>Local command execution:</strong> Permission profiles govern sandboxed commands that run on your machine. Connectors, MCP servers, browser or computer-use surfaces, Codex cloud environment settings, and approved escalations use their own controls.</li> <li><strong>Filesystem writes:</strong> A write-capable profile can create persistent changes. Treat writes to scripts, build steps, package manager hooks, shell startup files, and shared directories as sensitive because later tools or users can execute those files outside the original sandbox context.</li> <li><strong>Outbound destinations:</strong> Network domain rules constrain where sandboxed command traffic can go only while the network proxy is active. They do not determine whether an allowed destination is trustworthy, and wildcard allow rules stay broad.</li> <li><strong>Local services:</strong> An active network proxy blocks local and private network targets by default. Allowlisting <code>localhost</code>, private IPs, Unix sockets, or setting <code>allow_local_binding = true</code> explicitly opens access to local services.</li> </ul> <h3 id=\"what-the-network-proxy-does-not-control\">What the network proxy does not control</h3> <p>The network proxy only filters traffic from local commands that run inside the sandbox. It does not apply the profile's domain allowlist to:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Web search:</strong> The hosted search tool uses its own access settings. Use <code>web_search</code> and, for managed clients, <code>allowed_web_search_modes</code> to control it. <code>tools.web_search.allowed_domains</code> filters search results, not command network access.</li> <li><strong>Apps and connectors:</strong> Connector-backed tools use their own service-side connections, workspace permissions, and app or tool settings.</li> <li><strong>MCP servers:</strong> Local and remote MCP servers use their own process or transport. Control them with <code>mcp_servers</code> configuration and managed server allowlists.</li> <li><strong>Browser and Computer Use:</strong> Browser navigation and computer-use actions use their own feature and approval controls.</li> <li><strong>Codex service traffic:</strong> Model, authentication, and other client service requests use the client's separate HTTP and system-proxy settings.</li> <li><strong>Codex Cloud:</strong> These tasks use their environment's own <a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1?anchor=connect-to-services\">network settings</a>. Codex Cloud (Legacy) has separate <a href=\"#/article/a-b99b352f5f4ffa98\">internet access settings</a>.</li> </ul> <p>To limit these surfaces, configure each capability directly. A command network allowlist is not a global network policy for every action Codex can perform.</p> <h3 id=\"how-enforcement-works\">How enforcement works</h3> <ul> <li>On macOS, Codex uses Seatbelt sandbox profiles. If the selected policy cannot be enforced by the platform sandbox, Codex refuses to run the command instead of silently running it unsandboxed.</li> <li>On Linux and WSL, Codex uses <a href=\"https://github.com/containers/bubblewrap\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">bubblewrap (online)</a> and <a href=\"https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/userspace-api/seccomp_filter.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">seccomp (online)</a> to enforce filesystem restrictions. The legacy Landlock option can't isolate app-server Unix sockets and is rejected for filesystem-restricted policies. Disable <code>features.use_legacy_landlock</code> when upgrading. Hosts that block the required bubblewrap operations can't run these policies.</li> <li>On Windows, MXC uses process isolation for filesystem and network permissions. Review <a href=\"#/article/a-d86b339c4d76bce2?anchor=mxc-compatibility\">MXC compatibility</a> before enabling it. The legacy <code>elevated</code> fallback uses dedicated lower-privilege sandbox users, filesystem permission boundaries, and firewall rules. <code>unelevated</code> is a legacy fallback with weaker network isolation and cannot enforce every split read/write carveout, so unsupported policies are refused. Use WSL when you need the Linux sandbox model.</li> </ul> <h3 id=\"operational-guidance\">Operational guidance</h3> <p>Choose the narrowest profile that still lets the task complete, especially when you grant writes or outbound network access. Keep approval policy, secret handling, and allow rules aligned with that access level.</p> <h2 id=\"common-profiles\">Common profiles</h2> <h3 id=\"read-only-with-network-allowlist\">Read-only with network allowlist</h3> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"readonly-net\"\n\n[features]\nnetwork_proxy = true\n\n[permissions.readonly-net.filesystem]\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.readonly-net.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.readonly-net.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[permissions.readonly-net.network.domains]\n\"api.openai.com\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <h3 id=\"file-access-limited-to-workspace\">File access limited to workspace</h3> <p>Here is an example of a permission profile that will make your workspace folders writable by Codex while denying reads to the rest of the filesystem (with limited exceptions, as determined by <code>:minimal</code>).</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"workspace-only\"\n\n[permissions.workspace-only]\n# By extending the :workspace profile, you get Codex's safeguards to ensure\n# paths such as .git/, .agents/, .codex/, and .aws/ within a workspace root\n# are read-only while the rest of the folder is writable.\nextends = \":workspace\"\n\n[permissions.workspace-only.filesystem]\n# By default, deny read access to all files on disk.\n\":root\" = \"deny\"\n\n# Though in practice, a software agent needs to be able to read folders that\n# contain common tools, such as `/usr/bin`, to get work done, so grant access\n# to a \"minimal\" set of files and folders, as determined by Codex.\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n# By extending the :workspace profile, :tmpdir and :slash_tmp are \"write\" by\n# default, though you can deny access to them altogether, if desired.\n\":tmpdir\" = \"deny\"\n\":slash_tmp\" = \"deny\"\n</code></pre> <h3 id=\"workspace-write-without-network\">Workspace write without network</h3> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"project-edit\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem]\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"write\"\n\n[permissions.project-edit.network]\nenabled = false\n</code></pre> <h3 id=\"workspace-write-with-public-web-access\">Workspace write with public web access</h3> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">default_permissions = \"workspace-net\"\n\n[features]\nnetwork_proxy = true\n\n[permissions.workspace-net.filesystem]\n\":minimal\" = \"read\"\n\n[permissions.workspace-net.filesystem.\":workspace_roots\"]\n\".\" = \"write\"\n\n[permissions.workspace-net.network]\nenabled = true\n\n[permissions.workspace-net.network.domains]\n\"*\" = \"allow\"\n</code></pre> <p>Use the global <code>\"*\"</code> allow rule only when you intend to allow public network access. Deny rules can narrow a broad allowlist.</p>"};
